Here is an example os how to use this library. There are a sample
server and client to try it, wich uses a sample endpoint.

First of all, you will need to build the library:

 $ make -C src
 [...]

And then make available both the library and the endpoint, exporting
the correct environment variables. To make it easy, there is a shell
script wich you can 'source', like this:

 $ cd example/
 $ . setup.sh

Of course, you can, too, set it manually: to set the LD_LIBRARY_PATH:

 $ cd src/cpp/
 $ export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$(pwd)

and for PYTHONPATH:

 $ cd src/python
 $ export PYTHONPATH=$(pwd)
 $ cd ../../example
 $ export PYTHONPATH=$PYTHONPATH:$(pwd)

After that, you can launch the server (a simple Ice.Object wich
overwrites the ice_ping() method):

 $ ./server_datagram.py

and its client:

 $ ./client_datagram.py

If all works, you could see a message from servant implementation,
saying that ice_ping() method was called.



